>>__ load "simpExtLib";
>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `filter_rewrites`

``` hol4
simpExtLib.filter_rewrites :
  ((simpExtLib.thname option * thm) -> bool) ->
  simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
simpExtLib.filter_rewrites_preserving_controls :
  ((simpExtLib.thname option * thm) -> bool) ->
  simpExtLib.simpset -> simpExtLib.simpset
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Removes the rewrites that a predicate rejects from a tracked extension
simpset. There is no corresponding ordinary `simpLib` export.

`filter_rewrites keep ss` removes every rewrite `(name, th)`, as it was
added to `ss`, for which `keep` returns false. Everything else in `ss`
is kept, including the exclusions, limit, rewrite maker and strategy that
rebuilding from `ssfrags_of ss` would lose. This filters fragment inputs,
not individual compiled conjuncts; use `suspend_rewrite_sources` to suspend
exact compiled occurrences and their views.

When filtering changes the history, `filter_rewrites` replays it and resets
declared bounded allowances. `filter_rewrites_preserving_controls` instead
shares compatible surviving sources' actual controls with the input,
including exhausted counters and aliases. Both return the input itself
without replay if nothing is rejected; neither raises `Conv.UNCHANGED`.
Removed or incompatible sources invalidate their installed views. Select
tracked construction before installing rules; filtering cannot import an
ordinary simpset or recover its source identities.

### Failure

The predicate and replay callbacks can raise exceptions.

### Example

``` hol4
>> fun keep (_, th) = not (aconv (concl th) (concl AND_CLAUSES));
>>_ val ext_bool_ss = simpExtLib.lift_simpset boolSimps.bool_ss;
>>+ simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV
      (simpExtLib.filter_rewrites keep ext_bool_ss) [] “p ∧ T”;
>> simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV
     (simpExtLib.filter_rewrites keep ext_bool_ss) [] “p ∨ F”;
```

